{
  "id": "COMPOUND:29444 - PROTEIN:6138",
  "verbalization": "Laxanthone-I is implicated in the regulation of the 26S proteasome non-ATPase regulatory subunit 14 through a series of molecular interactions involving sialyltransferases. The evidence suggests that Laxanthone-I acts as a target for multiple sialyltransferases, which in turn are targets of other compounds, such as (1R,8S,9R,16R)-8,16-bis(4-hydroxyphenyl)-9-[(1R,8S,9R,16R)-4,6,12-trihydroxy-8,16-bis(4-hydroxyphenyl)-15-oxatetracyclo[8.6.1.02,7.014,17]heptadeca-2(7),3,5,10(17),11,13-hexaen-9-yl]-15-oxatetracyclo[8.6.1.02,7.014,17]heptadeca-2(7),3,5,10(17),11,13-hexaene-4,6,12-triol and 6'-O-Z-Para-Methoxycinnamoylharpagide. These sialyltransferases are likely involved in the modification of the proteasome regulatory subunit 14, potentially affecting its function. The presence of multiple pathways linking Laxanthone-I to the proteasome subunit underscores a possible role in modulating proteasome activity, though the exact mechanism remains to be fully elucidated. The evidence supports a mechanistic link between Laxanthone-I and the proteasome subunit, but further investigation is needed to confirm the specific interaction and its biological relevance.",
